metro police officer charleston  hartfield as you'll remember went to the  route 91 harvest festival as a fan back  in 2017 he was off duty he was there  with his wife when the shooting started  he jumped into action and he lost his  life in the process for the last two  years his service and his bravery has  been at the center of a charity hockey  game in hartfield's honor i was there  that his son isaiah agreed to be  interviewed for the first time  mid-september at lifeguard arena it was  packed on the ice veterans in white  first responders in blue at center ice  the real reason everyone showed up to  support the family of charleston  hartfield my father was a great man  isaiah hartfield is his son he lived to  serve everyone around him his community  his children his family officer  charleston hartfield army veteran  charleston hartfield he gave his life in  the one october shooting  on this night they play for the  hartfield cup means the world to me  honestly to see my dad's name carried on  in such a  an amazing fashion it's been four years  since isaiah lost his father at first it  was difficult to  move on four years later it's still  difficult honestly  especially when when this time of year  comes around emotions start to come back  up but for the most part we we're  getting along pretty well isaiah now  attends the university of oregon he  might try and walk onto the football  team next year but more importantly he's  turning this trauma into a lifetime  lesson it gives me a legacy to look up  to it it really kind of puts my  perspective in check you know it it um  it really broadens my horizon and it  reminds me to be a better person every  day because i'm not only living for  myself but i'm looking to kind of carry  on his name as well  well today isaiah says the hartfield  family will be with close friends to  honor their loss they'll also go to the  healing garden to pay their  respects they go there every year
 

